"""GP-150 write payload construction — no MIDI dependency, hardware-independent
logic split out of patch/gp150_write_replay.py (the live script) so it can be
unit-tested without rtmidi/pyobjc installed, matching gp150_wire.py/
gp150_catalog.py's pattern.

CAPTURED_WRITE_CHUNKS is copied VERBATIM (via plistlib, programmatically — a
first hand-transcription attempt introduced a real bug, two chunks silently
short by a few bytes each, caught by a length check before anything was ever
sent to hardware) from a real Suite write:
re/gp150_captures/write_attempts/write_import_slot200_clean.mmon (Suite
importing 006-UK900 DIST.prst to slot 199, 0-indexed). See
design/GP150_SUPPORT.md §3.1.

`build_chunks()` makes exactly two kinds of controlled, understood edits to
that capture — repointing the target slot, and/or overwriting the checksum
— confined entirely to chunk 0. Everything else, every other byte of every
other chunk, is untouched. Confirmed live 2026-09-03, twice:

  verify (unmodified, slot 199): round-tripped perfectly — read-back matched
  the source file exactly except the (expected) slot-index byte.

  corrupt (slot 197, checksum forced to 0,0): REJECTED by the device — the
  slot's prior content was completely unchanged on read-back, and the
  device's own ack had a visibly different shape (two 16-byte messages)
  than the successful write's ack (an 8-byte then a 24-byte message). This
  settles GP150-6's open checksum question: the device actively validates
  this field and refuses a write with an invalid one, it does not just
  store whatever is sent.
"""

